-- title:
--   Gauss valuation subring of L·ℚ(X(Γ)) inside L((q))
-- statement:
--   Let $\Gamma$ be an arbitrary subgroup of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$, let $L$ be a field of characteristic $0$, and let $K$ be an intermediate field of the extension $L \subseteq L((q))$ of Laurent series which is assumed equal to [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ Γ)`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103): that is, $K$ is the $L$-subfield of $L((q))$ generated by the coefficientwise image, under the map $\mathbb Q((q)) \to L((q))$ induced by $\mathbb Q \to L$, of the $\mathbb Q$-subfield of $\mathbb Q((q))$ generated by all quotients $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,p_f / \mathrm{intSeriesC}\,p_g$ with nonzero denominator, where $f,g$ run over modular forms of a common weight $k$ for $\Gamma$ (viewed in $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb R)$) and $p_f, p_g \in \mathbb Z[[q]]$ are integral $q$-expansions of $f$ and $g$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation ring with fraction field $L$, acting on $K$ compatibly through $L$, and let $j \in K$ be a nonzero element whose image in $L((q))$ is the coefficientwise image of $q^{-1}\cdot \mathrm{jNumQ}$, the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant. Then there exists a valuation subring $W_0$ of $K$ such that: (i) an element $f \in K$ lies in $W_0$ if and only if there are $x, y \in A[[q]]$ with $y$ having nonzero reduction modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ and $f \cdot y = x$ in $L((q))$ (images under $A[[q]] \to L[[q]] \subseteq L((q))$); (ii) $W_0$ contains the image of $A$; (iii) every element of the maximal ideal of $A$ maps into the non-units of $W_0$; (iv) for every $P \in A[X]$ whose reduction is nonzero, both $P(j)$ and $P(j)^{-1}$ lie in $W_0$; and (v) for any $f \in K$ and any $x, y \in A[[q]]$ with $y$ of nonzero reduction and $f \cdot y = x$ in $L((q))$, the element $f$ is a non-unit of $W_0$ precisely when the reduction of $x$ is zero.
--
--   This is the Gauss valuation on the base-changed $q$-expansion function field of $X(\Gamma)$: the valuation attached to reduction of $q$-expansions with bounded denominators, whose residue behaviour is controlled by the reduction of numerator and denominator power series, together with the statement that $A$ lies in the valuation ring and that polynomials in $j$ with nonzero reduction are units. No hypothesis on $\Gamma$ beyond being a subgroup is imposed; the result is used throughout the construction of integral models and the analysis of reductions of modular curves, for instance in the study of Igusa valuations and of chart algebras at full level.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_exists_gaussValuationSubring_laurentBaseChange_qExpFunctionFieldC.lean

